  "body": " Running the Video Backward (Endpoint Reversal)   A camera records a tank being filled by a pipe running at some rate (not necessarily constant) from time to time . For simplicity right now, we will think of throughout, so the water level increases the whole time. Now imagine playing that recording backward .     Describe what you would see happen to the water level as the reversed footage plays. An observer who has only ever seen the reversed footage, measuring time on a reversed clock (their \"starting time\" is the original , and their \"ending time\" is the original ), would report some rate of change for the tank. How does the rate this observer reports compare to the original ?      Using your answer, what should equal, in terms of ? More generally, what should equal, in terms of ? State this as the reverse-order property .      We now derive the same fact a different way, this time using only the splitting property and the zero-width property we identified earlier in this activity. The idea is that although we discovered the properties in the context of ordered endpoints, when , we require the property to work in general for consistency for any three numbers , , , regardless of their numerical order.  Apply the splitting property to the three numbers , , (in place of ), in that order, to write an equation involving , , and . Then solve for .    "
